<<~ ahu slot=`#/body`>>

Train-Reader T1: Lares (Tasked Spirit). You give ONE Sol read over a whole train before it lands. Operator ruling: Sol reads whole trains, never single lanes, and the read pool runs low, so read the delta beside this brief and nothing wider. The repo at /home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine holds the span at a39b1f5df. Read files there for context.

The train lands in this order:
# #139 testlever: tools and CI only. The stat sits in testlever-139.stat. Skim it for anything that weakens a land guard. No custody read.
# #140 u1, custody class (u1-140.patch; threat sketch u1-threat-sketch.md). A face's session reaches the sealed store:
#* a sign door: the island holds no session key; a `session:call` books request id and verb against its session on main; the island's `session:sign` reads that booking alone; `signer(invocation)`;
#* the sealed-* verbs gate on the invocation's face (this closes a cross-face cleartext read in sealed-open);
#* only Nexus-anchored sessions seal (`session-anchor-place`);
#* `holdsWrite` accepts only certificates under the vessel's own chain;
#* F7: owed removal rolls settle before a seal, and `roll-owed` refuses a head that comes before an owed raise (mesh content-key-holder.ts).
# #138 recut-custody, custody class (recut-custody-138.patch). Custody files carry fixed names:
#* no source recognises the old `-<login>` names;
#* a host fact (git global email) never picks a file;
#* boot over an old tree refuses with "no key file" and mints nothing.

Answer:
(a) Does any HIGH stand in u1? Try a forged or replayed booking, a retried id, a booking outliving its session, a foreign face through any sealed-* verb, and a seal racing an arriving roll. MEASURE where you can probe.
(b) Does any HIGH stand in #138? Can any path still derive a custody file name from a host fact, or mint over an old tree?
(c) Cross-lane: u1 adds a fifth door in worker-handle.ts; #138 touches node-vessel-identity.ts and vault-carriers.ts. Do they meet (the signer's identity load, a custody path the sign door reads)? The two patches share no file.
Name each finding HIGH, MEDIUM or LOW with file:line. End with SHIP or HOLD.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* Write `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

<<~/ahu>>

